    FIXME(mundane): She asks you for a guild letter of introduction to the Trade Guild in Aldgate. You are a guild member. A sealed letter with a guild credential would get her through the gate and give her standing to request asylum. Without it she is a Kesharat deserter and Aldgate may not take her in. She is not asking you to endorse her theory. She is asking for a piece of paper. She will do the rest.
    # --- COLOR ---
    # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated use.
    # The tea in the brazier pot is cold, its dregs dark and sediment-heavy.
    # The ledger on the desk is written in the compact script of the plateau, with a fresh entry crossed out in red ink.
    # The pencil-stub on the desk is worn to a nub, its tip split and stained with ink.
    # The copy of the anomalous message is folded and tucked into the inner pocket of her tunic, its edges frayed from handling.
    # The clay water jug beside the brazier is cracked, its rim chipped where it was dropped during the storm.
    # --- end ---
    # --- FACTUAL ---
    # She sets the cold tea in the brazier pot aside, the dregs dark and sediment-heavy, and reaches into the inner pocket of her tunic, pulling out a folded sheet of paper with frayed edges from repeated handling. The brass cylinder on her desk, a Kesharat-issue cipher seal worn smooth from repeated use, sits beside the ledger written in the compact script of the plateau, a fresh entry crossed out in red ink. The pencil-stub on the desk is worn to a nub, its tip split and stained with ink. She places the folded message on the desk, the paper thin from being passed from hand to hand, and says the words in a quiet voice: a sealed letter with a guild credential would get her through the gate and give her standing to request asylum. Without it she is a Kesharat deserter and Aldgate may not take her in. She is not asking you to endorse her theory. She is asking for a piece of paper. She will do the rest. The clay water jug beside the brazier is cracked, its rim chipped where it was dropped during the storm.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
    # She set the cold tea in the brazier pot aside—the dregs dark as old blood, thick with sediment—then reached into the inner pocket of her tunic, drawing forth a sheet of paper frayed at the edges from constant handling, as though it had been passed from hand to hand through a thousand silent nights. Beside the ledger, written in the tight, angular script of the plateau, the brass cylinder of the Kesharat cipher seal lay dull and worn smooth by years of use, its surface etched with the faint, ghostly grooves of countless turns. A single pencil-stub, worn to a nub, its tip split and stained with ink, lay beside the ledger, its end blackened as though it had been chewed in moments of strained thought. She placed the folded message upon the desk, the paper thin as a whisper, brittle with age and use, and spoke the words in a voice so low it seemed to dissolve into the silence of the room. A sealed letter bearing a guild credential would secure her passage through the gate and grant her the right to petition for asylum; without it, she was but a deserter of Kesharat, and Aldgate might refuse her. She was not asking you to affirm her theory, nor to believe in the truth of her tale—only to provide a piece of paper. She would do the rest. The clay water jug beside the brazier, cracked and chipped at the rim, bore the scar of a storm's fury, dropped and shattered in a moment of panic, now mended with threadbare resin and the quiet resignation of enduring things.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
    # She set the cold tea aside, the dregs thick and black as old blood in the brazier pot, and plucked the folded scrap of paper from the inner pocket of her tunic—frayed at the edges, worn thin from being passed from hand to hand, from pocket to pocket, like a talisman of desperate hope. Beside the ledger, its pages filled with the tight, precise script of the plateau, sat the brass cylinder of the Kesharat cipher seal, worn smooth by years of turning, its surface cool and slick to the touch. The pencil-stub on the desk was little more than a nub, its tip split and stained with ink, a relic of scribbled notes and half-formed plans. She laid the message upon the desk, the paper so thin it might tear at a breath, and spoke the words in a voice low as the wind through cracked clay. A sealed letter bearing a guild credential—just that—would open the gate, grant her standing, make her more than a nameless deserter in the eyes of Aldgate. Without it, she was nothing but a shadow with no name, a fugitive whose feet would be turned back at the wall. She was not asking you to believe her, not to embrace her dream. She only asked for a scrap of paper. The rest—she would carry it herself. The clay water jug by the brazier was cracked, its rim chipped where it had fallen in the storm, a silent testament to the night the world had cracked open.
    # --- end ---
