* Hear Ossal out = He finds you an hour later [requires !tag relay_post.heard_ossal_pitch]
    FIXME(mundane): Ossal does not mention the Lattice. He does not talk about theology or cosmic topology. He frames his concern in entirely secular terms. Devra is a state employee with access to classified communications. She is clearly disloyal. She has been keeping unauthorized copies of official documents. If she deserts the post the settlements downstream lose their only relay connection. Supply orders, medical requests, patrol coordination. Real people will suffer real consequences.
    # --- COLOR ---
    # The brass cylinder on Ossal's belt is engraved with the insignia of the Kesharat Inspection Corps, its surface worn smooth by decades of handling.
    # The ledger on the desk beside Ossal's chair contains entries in the smooth, balanced script of the regional office, with a section marked "Pending Reconciliation" in red ink.
    # A Kesharat-issued water skin sits on the floor near Ossal's feet, its leather cracked and its cap missing, the contents long evaporated.
    # The wire receiver on Devra's desk has a loose connection that emits a faint, continuous hum when the storm is at its worst.
    # The official file of transcriptions is bound in gray cloth with a metal clasp, its pages neatly indexed but missing the last six months of entries.
    # Ossal's hands rest on the arms of his chair, the knuckles swollen from years of writing, the nails clean and trimmed to a precise length.
    # --- end ---
    # --- FACTUAL ---
    # Ossal sits in his chair with his hands resting on the arms, knuckles swollen, nails clean and trimmed to a precise length. The brass cylinder on his belt bears the insignia of the Kesharat Inspection Corps, worn smooth by decades of handling. The ledger beside him holds entries in the smooth, balanced script of the regional office, a section marked "Pending Reconciliation" in red ink. A Kesharat-issued water skin lies on the floor near his feet, leather cracked, cap missing, contents long evaporated. The wire receiver on Devra's desk emits a faint, continuous hum from its loose connection, audible only when the storm's hiss drops to a whisper. The official file of transcriptions rests on the desk, bound in gray cloth with a metal clasp, pages neatly indexed but missing the last six months of entries. He speaks carefully, choosing words with the precision of a man who has written reports his entire life. Devra is a state employee with access to classified communications. She is clearly disloyal. She has been keeping unauthorized copies of official documents. If she deserts the post, the settlements downstream lose their only relay connection. Supply orders, medical requests, patrol coordination. Real people will suffer real consequences.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
    # Ossal sat in his chair, hands resting upon the arms with a stiffness that spoke of arthritic knots and long habit, the knuckles swollen like old roots, the nails clipped to a precision that bordered on the obsessive. The brass cylinder at his belt bore the insignia of the Kesharat Inspection Corps—its surface worn to a dull sheen by decades of constant handling, a patina of use that had smoothed even the sharpest edges of its design. Beside him, the ledger lay open, its pages filled with entries in the smooth, balanced script of the regional office, each line a testament to bureaucratic rigor. A single red-inked section marked "Pending Reconciliation" stood out like a wound in the otherwise ordered text. Near his feet, a Kesharat-issued water skin lay on the floor, its leather cracked and dry, the cap missing, the contents long vanished into the dust of the air. On Devra's desk, the wire receiver emitted a low, persistent hum—faint, almost imperceptible—only audible when the storm's hiss dropped to a whisper, as though the machine itself were listening. The official file of transcriptions rested upon the desk, bound in gray cloth, its metal clasp cold and unyielding; the pages were neatly indexed, each entry accounted for—except for the last six months, which had been excised as if by a deliberate hand. Ossal spoke carefully, each word chosen with the precision of a man who had spent his life crafting reports, weighing syllables as though they might crack under the weight of implication. Devra was a state employee with access to classified communications—her clearance was high, her position vital. But she was clearly disloyal. She had been keeping unauthorized copies of official documents, hidden in plain sight. If she deserted the post, the settlements downstream would lose their only relay connection—their sole thread to the outer world. Supply orders would go unanswered. Medical requests would vanish into silence. Patrol coordination would collapse. Real people would suffer. Real consequences. Not abstractions. Not statistics. Flesh and blood, trembling in the dark.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
    # Ossal sat in his chair, hands resting upon the arms, knuckles swollen like ancient knots in old wood, nails clipped to a precise, unyielding length. The brass cylinder at his belt bore the insignia of the Kesharat Inspection Corps—worn smooth by decades of touch, by the weight of duty, by the slow grind of years. Beside him, the ledger lay open, its pages filled with the smooth, balanced script of the regional office, each entry a measured breath of order. A section marked "Pending Reconciliation" stood out in red ink, sharp as a wound. On the floor, a Kesharat-issued water skin lay cracked and dry, cap long gone, the leather withered like a dead leaf. The wire receiver on Devra's desk hummed faintly, a low, persistent drone—audible only when the storm's hiss dipped to a whisper, as if the machine itself were listening. The official file of transcriptions rested on the desk, bound in gray cloth, clasped shut by a tarnished metal clasp. The pages were neatly indexed, but the last six months were gone—ripped out, erased, vanished. He spoke with care, each word chosen like a stone in a dry riverbed, precise, unyielding, shaped by a lifetime of reports. Devra was a state employee, yes—accessed the inner channels, knew the codes, the frequencies, the silent rhythms of the relay. But she was disloyal. She had kept unauthorized copies, hidden them in the folds of her desk, in the hollows of the old server casing. If she walked away, if she left the post, the settlements downstream would be cut off—no supply orders, no medical requests, no patrols to warn of raiders or sickness. Real people, real lives, would bleed into silence.
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC factual ---
    # (no findings)
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
    # [CRIT] "stiffness that spoke of arthritic knots and long habit" — INVENTION of arthritic condition and habitual posture beyond "hands resting on the arms."
    # [LOW] "clipped to a precision that bordered on the obsessive" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD states "trimmed to a precise length"; "obsessive" introduces a psychological interpretation not in the source.
    # [LOW] "a patina of use that had smoothed even the sharpest edges of its design" — INVENTION. The RECORD says the cylinder is "worn smooth by decades of handling" but does not specify that edges were originally sharp or detail the smoothing process as a design feature.
    # [LOW] "each line a testament to bureaucratic rigor" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. Adds interpretive weight ("testament," "rigor") beyond the RECORD's neutral "entries in the smooth, balanced script."
    # [CRIT] "stood out like a wound in the otherwise ordered text" — INVENTION. The metaphor of "wound" introduces emotional/symbolic content not present in the RECORD, which merely states the section is "marked...in red ink."
    # [LOW] "the contents long vanished into the dust of the air" — INVENTION of specific evaporation method ("dust of the air"). The RECORD states only "contents long evaporated."
    # [LOW] "as though the machine itself were listening" — INVENTION. Personification not supported by the RECORD, which describes only a "faint, continuous hum."
    # [CRIT] "which had been excised as if by a deliberate hand" — INVENTION. The RECORD states the pages are "missing the last six months of entries" but does not indicate deliberate removal or agency ("excised," "deliberate hand").
    # [LOW] "weighing syllables as though they might crack under the weight of implication" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD says he speaks "with the precision of a man who has written reports"; the draft adds a metaphor of fragility/danger not in the source.
    # [LOW] "her clearance was high, her position vital" — INVENTION. The RECORD states she "has access to classified communications" and is "a state employee" but does not specify clearance level or characterize her position as "vital."
    # [CRIT] "hidden in plain sight" — INVENTION. The RECORD states she "has been keeping unauthorized copies" but contains no detail about their location or concealment method.
    # [LOW] "their sole thread to the outer world" — INVENTION. The RECORD specifies "relay connection" but does not characterize it as a "thread to the outer world."
    # [CRIT] "Not abstractions. Not statistics. Flesh and blood, trembling in the dark." — PC INTERIORITY / INVENTION. These are interpretive moralizing statements not grounded in the RECORD's factual exposition. The final phrase "trembling in the dark" is a new sensory/emotional claim.
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC voiced REH mundane ---
    # [CRIT] "knuckles swollen like ancient knots in old wood" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT introducing unspecified visual comparison.
    # [CRIT] "worn smooth by decades of touch, by the weight of duty, by the slow grind of years" — INVENTION adding causal narrative ("weight of duty," "slow grind") beyond "worn smooth by decades of handling."
    # [CRIT] "each entry a measured breath of order" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT personifying the ledger entries with unspecified metaphor.
    # [CRIT] "sharp as a wound" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT adding sensory/emotional claim not in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "withered like a dead leaf" — INVENTION adding visual comparison beyond "cracked."
    # [CRIT] "as if the machine itself were listening" — INVENTION attributing agency/interiority to the receiver.
    # [CRIT] "ripped out, erased, vanished" — CONTRADICTION. RECORD states pages are "missing"; DRAFT claims active removal ("ripped out").
    # [CRIT] "each word chosen like a stone in a dry riverbed, precise, unyielding, shaped by a lifetime of reports" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT introducing elaborate metaphor and unspecified emotional/intentional claim.
    # [CRIT] "accessed the inner channels, knew the codes, the frequencies, the silent rhythms of the relay" — INVENTION adding technical specifics and knowledge beyond RECORD's "access to classified communications."
    # [CRIT] "hidden them in the folds of her desk, in the hollows of the old server casing" — INVENTION specifying locations and physical details of document storage.
    # [CRIT] "no patrols to warn of raiders or sickness" — INVENTION adding specific hazards ("raiders or sickness") beyond RECORD's "patrol coordination."
    # [CRIT] "Real people, real lives, would bleed into silence" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT adding poetic/emotional intensification ("bleed into silence") beyond factual consequence claim.
    # --- end ---

    FIXME(mundane): He asks you to help him detain her until a patrol arrives. He is too old to confront her alone. With your help he could secure the post, confiscate the copies, and keep the relay operational until a replacement operator arrives. He frames this as a civic duty.
    # --- COLOR ---
    # The brass teapot on the brazier is dented from travel and bears a faint scorch mark near the spout.
    # The wire receiver on the desk has a loose connection that emits a low hum when touched.
    # The ledger on the desk is written in the compact script of the plateau with a fresh entry crossed out in ink.
    # The Kesharat-issued horse tethered outside the back door is not properly secured and shifts uneasily in the wind.
    # The signal tower scaffolding on the roof is half-built and casts a long shadow across the stone floor.
    # The clay water jug on the table has a wet cloth wrapped around its neck, though the water inside is nearly gone.
    # --- end ---
    # --- FACTUAL ---
    # The brass teapot on the brazier is dented from travel and bears a faint scorch mark near the spout. The wire receiver on the desk has a loose connection that emits a low hum when touched. The ledger on the desk is written in the compact script of the plateau with a fresh entry crossed out in ink. The Kesharat-issued horse tethered outside the back door is not properly secured and shifts uneasily in the wind. The signal tower scaffolding on the roof is half-built and casts a long shadow across the stone floor. The clay water jug on the table has a wet cloth wrapped around its neck, though the water inside is nearly gone. The back room door remains closed; light leaks from under it in a thin line. Ossal steps into the main room, his movements deliberate, his voice measured. He asks you to help him detain her until a patrol arrives. He is too old to confront her alone. With your help he could secure the post, confiscate the copies, and keep the relay operational until a replacement operator arrives. He frames this as a civic duty.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
    # The brass teapot upon the brazier bore the dents of long travel, its surface pitted with the scars of arduous journeys, and near the spout a faint scorch mark marred its once-gleaming surface—a relic of some forgotten fire. On the desk, the wire receiver hummed with a low, persistent drone when touched, as though the air itself trembled with unspoken messages. The ledger lay open, its pages filled with the cramped, angular script of the plateau; a fresh entry, hastily written, was crossed out with a single, jagged line of ink, as if some terrible thought had been suppressed mid-sentence. Outside the back door, the Kesharat-issued horse stood tethered by a frayed rope, its halter loose and ill-fastened, shifting restlessly in the wind as though it sensed some unseen dread. On the roof, the scaffold of the signal tower rose half-completed, its timbers jutting like broken ribs into the sky, casting a long, crooked shadow across the stone floor below. The clay water jug on the table, wrapped in a damp cloth to preserve what little moisture remained, was nearly empty—only a film of sediment clung to its base. The back room door stood shut, sealed against the world, yet a thin line of light seeped beneath it, faint and persistent, as though something within refused to be forgotten. Then Ossal stepped into the main room, moving with the careful deliberation of one who has walked through too many nights without sleep. His voice was low, measured, as though each syllable were weighed before release. "You must help me detain her," he said. "Until the patrol arrives." He did not say it with fear, but with a terrible certainty—older than the stones, older than the silence. "I am too old to face her alone." His words were not a plea, but a burden laid upon the shoulders of another. "With your help, we may secure the post, seize the copies, and keep the relay running until a new operator comes." He spoke of duty—the civic duty of the watch, of the keep, of the unbroken chain. But in the quiet between the syllables, there lay something else—something older, deeper, unnameable.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
    # The brass teapot on the brazier bore the dents of long travel and a faint scorch mark near the spout, as if it had once danced too close to the flame. The wire receiver on the desk hummed low and steady when touched, a thin, insistent drone like a wasp trapped in glass. The ledger lay open, its pages filled in the tight, precise script of the plateau, one entry freshly crossed out in ink—hasty, furious, as though the hand that wrote it had paused mid-sentence and then torn it away. Outside the back door, the Kesharat-issued horse shifted in the wind, its tether loose and frayed, its ears flicking at every breath of the night. The scaffold of the signal tower loomed half-finished on the roof, its shadow stretching across the stone floor like a warning. The clay water jug on the table held only a thin film of liquid, the cloth wrapped around its neck long since dried and brittle. The door to the back room stood shut, but a sliver of light seeped beneath it, thin and sharp as a knife-edge. Then Ossal stepped into the room—measured, deliberate, his boots silent on the stone. His voice was low, grave, like the toll of a bell in a forgotten town. "I need your hand," he said. "She must not pass. Not until the patrol comes. I am too old for such a fight. But with you, I can hold the post, seize the copies, keep the relay alive until a new operator arrives. This is not for me. It is for the line. For the chain. For the silence that must not be broken."
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC factual ---
    # [LOW] "The back room door remains closed; light leaks from under it in a thin line." — This detail about the door and light is invented; it does not appear in the BEAT, BULLETS, BRIEF, or LOCALE GUIDE.
    # [LOW] "Ossal steps into the main room, his movements deliberate, his voice measured." — "his voice measured" is an interpretive characterization not grounded in observable action or the source material; the BRIEF describes his speech as "careful" and "precise" in word choice, not vocal tone.
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
    # [CRIT] "pitted with the scars of arduous journeys" — INVENTION: no detail about pitting or multiple journeys in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "some forgotten fire" — INVENTION: causation of scorch mark is not specified in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "as though the air itself trembled with unspoken messages" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: attributes meaning/sentience to the hum not present in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "hastily written" — INVENTION: no temporal detail about the crossing-out in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "as if some terrible thought had been suppressed mid-sentence" — PC INTERIORITY/INTERPRETATION: imputes emotional/narrative meaning to the crossed-out entry beyond the factual record.
    # [CRIT] "by a frayed rope" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD says "not properly secured" but does not specify "frayed rope."
    # [CRIT] "as though it sensed some unseen dread" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: attributes emotional/supernatural perception to the horse not in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "its timbers jutting like broken ribs into the sky" — INVENTION: anatomical metaphor adds a visual/symbolic claim not in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "crooked shadow" — INVENTION: RECORD says "long shadow," not "crooked."
    # [CRIT] "only a film of sediment clung to its base" — INVENTION: specific detail about sediment not in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "sealed against the world" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: anthropomorphizes the door with intentionality not in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "as though something within refused to be forgotten" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: introduces unspecified supernatural/metaphysical claim.
    # [CRIT] "moving with the careful deliberation of one who has walked through too many nights without sleep" — INVENTION: backstory/condition (sleep deprivation) not in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "as though each syllable were weighed before release" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: adds interpretive layer about internal deliberation beyond "measured."
    # [CRIT] "He did not say it with fear, but with a terrible certainty—older than the stones, older than the silence." — PC INTERIORITY: narrates Ossal's internal emotional state and invokes cosmic/supernatural register not supported by RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "His words were not a plea, but a burden laid upon the shoulders of another." — PC INTERIORITY: interprets Ossal's intent and frames the PC's receipt of his words as burden-bearing.
    # [CRIT] "He spoke of duty—the civic duty of the watch, of the keep, of the unbroken chain." — INVENTION: "the watch," "the keep," "unbroken chain" are institutional/architectural details not in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "But in the quiet between the syllables, there lay something else—something older, deeper, unnameable." — PC INTERIORITY: narrates the PC's perception of subtext and invokes an unspecified supernatural presence beyond the RECORD's "frames this as a civic duty."
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC voiced REH mundane ---
    # [CRIT] "as if it had once danced too close to the flame" — INVENTION: assigns causation and narrative history to the scorch mark not in RECORD.
    # [LOW] "a thin, insistent drone like a wasp trapped in glass" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: "insistent" and the wasp simile add sensory/emotional claim beyond "low hum."
    # [CRIT] "hasty, furious, as though the hand that wrote it had paused mid-sentence and then torn it away" — INVENTION: attributes emotional state and deliberate action to the crossing-out; RECORD only says "crossed out."
    # [CRIT] "its tether loose and frayed" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD says "not properly secured" but does not specify "frayed."
    # [LOW] "like a warning" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: assigns symbolic/ominous intent to the shadow.
    # [CRIT] "the cloth wrapped around its neck long since dried and brittle" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD states "wet cloth"; VOICED draft claims it is "dried and brittle."
    # [LOW] "thin and sharp as a knife-edge" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: "sharp" adds a quality not in RECORD's "thin line."
    # [CRIT] "his boots silent on the stone" — INVENTION: adds specific sensory detail not in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "His voice was low, grave, like the toll of a bell in a forgotten town" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: "grave" and bell-toll simile add emotional/tonal claims beyond "measured."
    # [CRIT] "She must not pass" — INVENTION: introduces a female antagonist and specific constraint not explicit in RECORD's "detain her."
    # [CRIT] "This is not for me. It is for the line. For the chain. For the silence that must not be broken." — INVENTION: Ossal's direct speech elaborates motivations and abstract concepts ("the line," "the chain," "the silence") not in RECORD's "civic duty" framing.
    # --- end ---

    FIXME(dread): You can see, beneath the careful phrasing, that he is afraid. Not of Devra. Of what it means if she is right. Detaining her is not just about keeping the relay open. It is about keeping the question closed.
    +add_tag relay_post.heard_ossal_pitch
