* Confront Devra openly = Help Ossal secure the post [requires tag relay_post.heard_ossal_pitch]
    FIXME(mundane): You tell Ossal you will help. He looks relieved in a way he tries not to show. He suggests you wake Devra together and tell her plainly what is going to happen until a patrol arrives. The two of you walk to her desk.
    # --- COLOR ---
    # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from frequent handling.
    # The stack of transcriptions beside her desk includes a folder labeled "Unverified Coordinates - Mesa Sector 7," its edges frayed from repeated opening.
    # The brazier in the corner holds embers that glow faintly under a thin layer of ash, its iron rim scorched from repeated use.
    # The wire receiver on the desk is cold, its earpiece cracked and partially disconnected, though the cord remains plugged into the wall junction.
    # A second notebook, bound in faded blue cloth, lies open beneath a stack of blank forms, its pages filled with Devra's cramped handwriting and marginal sketches of geometric patterns.
    # The cup of tea on the desk is cold, its surface filmed with a layer of dust that has settled since the storm began.
    # --- end ---
    # --- FACTUAL ---
    # You tell Ossal you will help. He looks relieved in a way he tries not to show. He suggests you wake Devra together and tell her plainly what is going to happen until a patrol arrives. The two of you walk to her desk. The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from frequent handling. The stack of transcriptions beside her desk includes a folder labeled "Unverified Coordinates - Mesa Sector 7," its edges frayed from repeated opening. The brazier in the corner holds embers that glow faintly under a thin layer of ash, its iron rim scorched from repeated use. The wire receiver on the desk is cold, its earpiece cracked and partially disconnected, though the cord remains plugged into the wall junction. A second notebook, bound in faded blue cloth, lies open beneath a stack of blank forms, its pages filled with Devra's cramped handwriting and marginal sketches of geometric patterns. The cup of tea on the desk is cold, its surface filmed with a layer of dust that has settled since the storm began.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
    # You tell Ossal you will help. He looks relieved—though not in the way one might expect, for his relief is a quiet, almost reluctant thing, as if he feared the weight of hope more than the burden of despair. He suggests you wake Devra together and speak plainly of what is to come, until the patrol arrives. As you move toward her desk, the air in the chamber seems still, thick with the lingering breath of the storm. The brass cylinder upon her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth by years of frantic handling, the engravings now blurred into a dull, ghostly pattern. Beside it, a stack of transcriptions lies half-spread, the topmost folder bearing the faded label: *Unverified Coordinates - Mesa Sector 7*, its edges frayed and frayed again, as though the pages had been opened and closed too often in moments of dread. In the corner, the brazier holds embers that glow faintly beneath a thin, ashen veil—its iron rim blackened and scarred from repeated exposure to heat and neglect. The wire receiver on the desk is cold to the touch, its earpiece cracked and partially severed, though the cord remains stubbornly plugged into the wall junction, as if waiting for a signal that will never come. Beneath a pile of blank forms lies a second notebook, bound in faded blue cloth, open to a page where Devra's cramped script snakes across the paper like a frantic incantation, interspersed with delicate, obsessive sketches of interlocking geometries—patterns that seem to shift if stared at too long. And on the desk, a cup of tea has long since gone cold, its surface film of dust settled not from neglect, but from the slow, unrelenting passage of time since the storm began—time that has not moved forward, but only deepened into silence.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
    # You tell Ossal you will help. His relief is a flicker in his eyes, quickly masked—too quickly, like a man who has learned not to trust ease. He nods, jaw tight, and says you should wake Devra together and speak plain, before the patrol arrives. You move toward her desk, boots silent on the worn floorboards. There, on the surface, the brass cylinder gleams dully—a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its face worn smooth from years of turning in nervous fingers. Beside it, a stack of transcriptions, the topmost folder worn thin at the edges, its label faded but still legible: *Unverified Coordinates - Mesa Sector 7*. The brazier in the corner holds a dying ember, red beneath a thin shroud of ash, its iron rim blackened and pitted from fire's kiss. The wire receiver sits cold on the desk—earpiece cracked, the cord half-loose from the wall junction, though still plugged in, as if waiting. Beneath a pile of blank forms lies a second notebook, bound in blue cloth long faded to the color of storm clouds. Its pages are filled with Devra's cramped script, lines of equations and notes, and in the margins, sketches—geometric patterns, spirals, and angles that seem to twist just out of sight. And beside it, the tea cup—long cold, its surface film of dust undisturbed since the storm broke.
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC factual ---
    # (no findings)
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
    # [CRIT] "though not in the way one might expect, for his relief is a quiet, almost reluctant thing, as if he feared the weight of hope more than the burden of despair" — PC INTERIORITY. Narrates Ossal's internal emotional state and motivations beyond the RECORD's "looks relieved in a way he tries not to show."
    # [CRIT] "the air in the chamber seems still, thick with the lingering breath of the storm" — INVENTION. No atmospheric description of air quality in the RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "the engravings now blurred into a dull, ghostly pattern" — INVENTION. The RECORD specifies the surface is "worn smooth" but does not mention engravings or their blurring.
    # [CRIT] "as though the pages had been opened and closed too often in moments of dread" — PC INTERIORITY / LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. Attributes emotional context (dread) to Devra's handling of the folder; the RECORD only states "repeated opening."
    # [CRIT] "its iron rim blackened and scarred from repeated exposure to heat and neglect" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT / CONTRADICTION. The RECORD says the rim is "scorched from repeated use"; "neglect" is a new interpretive claim not supported.
    # [CRIT] "its earpiece cracked and partially severed" — CONTRADICTION. The RECORD says "cracked and partially disconnected," not "severed."
    # [CRIT] "as if waiting for a signal that will never come" — PC INTERIORITY / LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. Attributes intention and futility to an inanimate object.
    # [CRIT] "like a frantic incantation, interspersed with delicate, obsessive sketches of interlocking geometries—patterns that seem to shift if stared at too long" — INVENTION. The RECORD mentions "marginal sketches of geometric patterns" but not frantic tone, obsessiveness, interlocking forms, or optical illusion effects.
    # [CRIT] "its surface film of dust settled not from neglect, but from the slow, unrelenting passage of time since the storm began—time that has not moved forward, but only deepened into silence" — PC INTERIORITY / LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. Attributes metaphysical and temporal meanings to dust; contradicts RECORD's straightforward "since the storm began."
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC voiced REH mundane ---
    # [CRIT] "His relief is a flicker in his eyes, quickly masked—too quickly, like a man who has learned not to trust ease." — PC INTERIORITY. Narrativizes Ossal's internal state and psychological history beyond the RECORD's "looks relieved in a way he tries not to show."
    # [CRIT] "jaw tight" — INVENTION. No jaw tension specified in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "boots silent on the worn floorboards" — INVENTION. No footstep sound or floor description in RECORD.
    # [CRIT] "the brass cylinder gleams dully" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. RECORD says surface is "worn smooth"; "gleams" adds a reflective property not stated.
